# This project has no build step or filename hashing, so every JS/CSS/HTML
# file is served under the same URL forever — a browser that caches those
# files too aggressively can end up running a broken MIX of old and new code
# after an update (e.g. an old Renderer.js without a method a newer scene
# file already calls), which reads as a random crash/freeze with no obvious
# cause.
#
# `no-cache` (NOT `no-store`) is the fix for that without giving up caching
# entirely: it still lets the browser cache the response, but forces a
# revalidation request (conditional GET, via ETag/Last-Modified) before ever
# reusing it — an unchanged file gets a cheap 304 with no body, a changed
# one gets the new bytes, so the exact "stale mixed code" bug above still
# can't happen. The previous `no-store, must-revalidate` combo instead
# forced a full re-download of every JS/CSS/HTML file on every single
# repeat visit, which is a real Lighthouse "efficient cache policy" hit with
# no correctness benefit over plain `no-cache` here.
#
# Note: this file only takes effect on an Apache host (e.g. this local XAMPP
# tree). If the production custom domain (see CNAME) is served via GitHub
# Pages instead, .htaccess is never processed there and this is inert —
# GitHub Pages already sends its own conditional-caching headers by default.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  <FilesMatch "\.(js|css|html)$">
    Header set Cache-Control "no-cache"
  </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>
